1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:02,230
Hello, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:02,230 --> 00:00:05,120
Welcome to the fifth episode of Earthlings
101.

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:43,270
Today we will talk about games, stories and
dreams.

4
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:47,479
As mentioned in the first episode, earthlings
don't have the tools and instincts animals

5
00:00:47,479 --> 00:00:48,690
have to survive.

6
00:00:48,690 --> 00:00:53,129
Instead, nature gave them a brain and the
capacity to learn and to create tools.

7
00:00:53,129 --> 00:00:58,570
Now, learning to survive, is kind of problematic,
because you can't learn from experience.

8
00:00:58,570 --> 00:01:04,239
As soon as you experience how not to survive,
you may be smarter, but you are also dead.

9
00:01:04,239 --> 00:01:07,829
The solution evolution found was something
called imagination.

10
00:01:07,829 --> 00:01:11,380
Basically, earthlings create a simplified
model of reality in their head and use it

11
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:13,960
to predict the outcome of different actions.

12
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:18,479
It's kind of a reality simulator, and it's
very flexible, powerful and fast.

13
00:01:18,479 --> 00:01:21,340
However, sometimes it's not fast enough.

14
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:26,090
When the earthling is in immediate danger,
for example, there is no time to imagine - the

15
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:28,450
earthling has to decide immediately or die.

16
00:01:28,450 --> 00:01:33,250
So, it would be great to be prepared for all
kinds of dangerous situations, by some kind

17
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:35,250
of training exercise.

18
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:38,729
Earthlings have actually developped three
mechanisms which might serve for exactly that

19
00:01:38,729 --> 00:01:42,899
purpose : Dreams, games, and stories.

20
00:01:42,899 --> 00:01:45,659
Dreams happen usually at night, during sleep.

21
00:01:45,659 --> 00:01:47,789
Earthlings are used to sleep every night.

22
00:01:47,789 --> 00:01:51,259
Sleeping means putting the body at rest and
shutting down both voluntary movements and

23
00:01:51,259 --> 00:01:52,659
sensory system.

24
00:01:52,659 --> 00:01:56,500
Nobody knows the purpose of sleep, but sure
is that the brain uses a part of this time

25
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:57,630
to dream.

26
00:01:57,630 --> 00:02:01,899
Dreams are somewhat similar to those propaganda
implants used in the Union of Galactic Communist

27
00:02:01,899 --> 00:02:02,899
Republics.

28
00:02:02,899 --> 00:02:07,609
A propaganda implant activates itself during
torpidity or hibernation and creates interactive

29
00:02:07,609 --> 00:02:09,629
propaganda hallucinations.

30
00:02:09,629 --> 00:02:13,870
To do so, it taps into both the sensory input
and the motory output to make the brain believe

31
00:02:13,870 --> 00:02:16,180
that the hallucination is actually happening.

32
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:19,730
Dreams are more or less the same, but without
the propaganda implant.

33
00:02:19,730 --> 00:02:24,769
They are somewhat chaotic simulations of physical
danger, social conflicts and other critical

34
00:02:24,769 --> 00:02:28,290
situations, created by the beast and thrown
at the ego during sleep.

35
00:02:28,290 --> 00:02:31,280
Dreams are typically random series of threatening
situations.

36
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:36,129
For example, the dreamer runs down a hallway
because he is late for work, and he is pursued

37
00:02:36,129 --> 00:02:40,379
by a predator, then he suddenly falls down
a trap and finds himself unprepared in a final

38
00:02:40,379 --> 00:02:44,939
school exam, during a zombie attack, and the
examiner is his mother, and everything happens

39
00:02:44,939 --> 00:02:49,220
on a sinking ship, and then he realizes that
he is naked.

40
00:02:49,220 --> 00:02:53,690
All those situations are either physical or
social danger situations, mixed randomly together

41
00:02:53,690 --> 00:02:54,950
by the beast.

42
00:02:54,950 --> 00:02:59,090
They may be actual concerns like being late
for work, past fears like his mother or his

43
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:04,970
final exam, as well as fears dating back to
prehistoric times like being chased by a predator.

44
00:03:04,970 --> 00:03:08,660
The actual problems occurring in dreams can
say a lot about the dreamer's unconscious

45
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:10,099
fears.

46
00:03:10,099 --> 00:03:14,250
Human brain-doctors are trained to question
patients about their dreams, to analyze the

47
00:03:14,250 --> 00:03:18,129
underlying fears, and then to come invariantly
to the conclusion that the patient wants to

48
00:03:18,129 --> 00:03:20,760
kill his father and to have sex with his mother.

49
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:21,920
This is called psychiatry.

50
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:27,930
Scientific advice: When you abduct humans
to experiment on them, you don't need an expensive

51
00:03:27,930 --> 00:03:29,349
memory erasing machine.

52
00:03:29,349 --> 00:03:33,930
Simply abduct humans in their sleep and put
them back into their beds when you are done.

53
00:03:33,930 --> 00:03:38,060
Even if they remember the experiments, they
will probably believe that it was just a bad

54
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:39,530
dream, and forget it.

55
00:03:39,530 --> 00:03:42,780
The second survival training mechanism is
called games.

56
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:44,670
Doing a game is called playing.

57
00:03:44,670 --> 00:03:48,250
Like dreams, games are interactive danger
simulations.

58
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:52,720
But unlike dreams, games are played when the
earthlings are awake, they can be shared,

59
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:55,690
and the earthlings know that there is no real
danger.

60
00:03:55,690 --> 00:03:59,849
Games are usually based on a set of more or
less strict rules which fix the playing ground,

61
00:03:59,849 --> 00:04:03,829
the equipment, the allowed actions and the
conditions for winning the game.

62
00:04:03,829 --> 00:04:08,030
Games are also full of symbols and rituals
- something we will learn about in a forthcoming

63
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:09,030
episode.

64
00:04:09,030 --> 00:04:13,170
They are many different kinds of games, but
they are all made of four basic ingredients:

65
00:04:13,170 --> 00:04:16,750
Challenge, chance, make-believe and vertigo.

66
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:20,820
Challenge means that the players compete with
each other , or with the game itself, to maximize

67
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:22,200
their score.

68
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:27,051
This usually means to outrun, outjump, outsmart
or out-wut-ever the other players, or the

69
00:04:27,051 --> 00:04:28,310
game itself.

70
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:32,230
This is the game variant of the evolutionary
principle "survival of the fittest".

71
00:04:32,230 --> 00:04:35,610
Chance means that many games include some
side of random elements.

72
00:04:35,610 --> 00:04:40,820
In most cases, the outcome is not completely
random, as chance is combined with challenge.

73
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:44,630
Those games are essentially training exercises
for dealing with random events.

74
00:04:44,630 --> 00:04:46,780
The third ingredient is make-believe.

75
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:51,200
The players pretend to be somebody else, and
the game equipment symbolizes things in real

76
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:52,200
life.

77
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:56,780
This allows simulating all kinds of situations
without need for realistic equipment.

78
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:01,220
Earthling imagination can transform cardboard
boxes into castles and ships, a house plant

79
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:05,910
into a weapon of mass destruction, sticks
and brooms into swords and lances, and a living

80
00:05:05,910 --> 00:05:08,740
room into the theater of epic battles.

81
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:12,650
The fourth, somewhat surprising element of
games is vertigo.

82
00:05:12,650 --> 00:05:16,950
This means disrupting the stability of perception
to create some kind of voluptuous panic.

83
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:21,790
The disruption can come from high altitude,
the thrill of speed, spinning around, horror,

84
00:05:21,790 --> 00:05:23,580
or other factors.

85
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:27,830
The evolutionary goal is possibly to train
the earthling to keep a clear mind in stress

86
00:05:27,830 --> 00:05:28,830
situations.

87
00:05:28,830 --> 00:05:32,300
Vertigo in the larger sense includes other
forms of dizziness, such as the thrill of

88
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:36,660
high stakes, blood lust, drunkenness or even
sexual arousal.

89
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:42,280
Not all games features all four ingredients,
challenge, chance, make-believe and vertigo.

90
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:45,760
But every game involves at least one of those
ingredients.

91
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:50,060
A popular kind of games are physical games,
also known as sports.

92
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:55,760
They involve, for example, sprinting, running
after objects in teams, throwing things, and

93
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:56,860
combat.

94
00:05:56,860 --> 00:06:01,660
Most sports are rather obvious training exercises
for melee combat, hunting, and running from

95
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:02,660
predators.

96
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:06,900
The funny thing is that all these abilities
are somewhat outdated, as modern earthlings

97
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:10,600
buy their meet at the butcher, fight with
firearms and have locked away all kinds of

98
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:13,090
dangerous creatures into zoos.

99
00:06:13,090 --> 00:06:18,190
Except for the microbes.

100
00:06:18,190 --> 00:06:19,940
Tips for tourists.

101
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:24,160
If you want to observe the variety of games,
visit an amusement park.

102
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:29,550
Here you'll find it all: Challenge and chance,
make-believe, and huge machines uniquely dedicated

103
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:33,030
to vertigo.

104
00:06:33,030 --> 00:06:38,240
For the alien observer, games look unpleasant
- like forced training exercises for mercenary

105
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:43,680
slaves: Combat training, tactical exercises,
centrifuge training and so on.

106
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:47,440
But the difference is that earthlings actually
like playing, as their brains are programmed

107
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:49,280
to enjoy games.

108
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:51,790
Now to the third mechanism : Stories.

109
00:06:51,790 --> 00:06:56,720
In a nutshell, stories are made-up accounts
on people in dangerous situations.

110
00:06:56,720 --> 00:07:01,380
Unlike dreams and games, stories are not interactive,
but they make the audience think: "What would

111
00:07:01,380 --> 00:07:03,530
I do in this situation?".

112
00:07:03,530 --> 00:07:08,580
Stories are transmitted in various ways : They
are told, played on stage, sung, written,

113
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:11,800
drawn or filmed - to name just some media.

114
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:14,870
Stories are often passed on from generation
to generation.

115
00:07:14,870 --> 00:07:18,910
Some famous stories are thousands of years
old and were transmitted through various media

116
00:07:18,910 --> 00:07:21,890
and translated into many languages.

117
00:07:21,890 --> 00:07:24,400
Many stories share a common structure.

118
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:27,760
Earthlings call this structure "the hero's
journey", or "the mono myth".

119
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:32,530
It goes like this: An earthling, called "hero",
lives his ordinary life, when he is suddenly

120
00:07:32,530 --> 00:07:36,530
called to adventure - often in form of a mighty
enemy threatening his world.

121
00:07:36,530 --> 00:07:40,940
He first refuses the call, but after a bit
of encouragement, he accepts it.

122
00:07:40,940 --> 00:07:45,220
He meets a mentor who gives him advice and
useful artefacts, and then enters an unknown

123
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:49,650
world, encounters foes, passes trials and
finds allies.

124
00:07:49,650 --> 00:07:53,830
Eventually he intrudes into the enemy's lair,
gets into the heart of the lair, faces the

125
00:07:53,830 --> 00:07:55,530
enemy and defeats him.

126
00:07:55,530 --> 00:07:59,750
Then he gets the enemy's treasure and makes
his way back to his world, often chased by

127
00:07:59,750 --> 00:08:00,750
foes.

128
00:08:00,750 --> 00:08:05,050
At the end of the journey, he lives a final
moment of danger, death and rebirth, and finally

129
00:08:05,050 --> 00:08:09,650
returns home, richer than before and master
of the two worlds.

130
00:08:09,650 --> 00:08:12,470
Not all stories stick strictly to this structure,
though.

131
00:08:12,470 --> 00:08:18,180
There are, for example, stories called tragedies
where the hero ultimately fails.

132
00:08:18,180 --> 00:08:21,300
But there is one point all stories have in
common: Conflict.

133
00:08:21,300 --> 00:08:27,700
So, odds are that preparing for conflicts
is the whole point of stories.

134
00:08:27,700 --> 00:08:29,080
Strategic advice.

135
00:08:29,080 --> 00:08:33,319
If you are planning to attack earth, with
a giant bioengineered dinosaur, stay clear

136
00:08:33,319 --> 00:08:35,190
from an island called Japan.

137
00:08:35,190 --> 00:08:39,019
Japanese stories have prepared the Japanese
for exactly this kind of attack since half

138
00:08:39,019 --> 00:08:42,560
a century, so they know to defend against
such a monster.

139
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:46,529
Better attack America, they are only prepared
against zombie attacks.

140
00:08:46,529 --> 00:08:51,851
Besides being instructions for dangerous situations,
stories have another, deeper aspect: Most

141
00:08:51,851 --> 00:08:56,850
of them feature myths and archetypes, ancient
ideas deeply anchored in the human mind.

142
00:08:56,850 --> 00:08:59,620
We will learn about myths in another episode.

143
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:04,839
Earthling cubs, also called children, spend
most of their time sleeping, playing games,

144
00:09:04,839 --> 00:09:06,350
and listening to stories.

145
00:09:06,350 --> 00:09:09,870
Childhood is a phase of life where earthlings
are still learning how to get along in the

146
00:09:09,870 --> 00:09:10,870
world.

147
00:09:10,870 --> 00:09:15,399
This is another hint that the purpose of dreams,
stories and games might be actually: learning.

148
00:09:15,399 --> 00:09:19,620
Anthropologists have a lot of theories about
the origin and purpose of games dreams and

149
00:09:19,620 --> 00:09:20,620
stories.

150
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:24,370
But fact is, all three mechanisms are all
about dangerous situations.

151
00:09:24,370 --> 00:09:29,490
So, when an earthling encounters a dangerous
situation, odds are that he already experienced

152
00:09:29,490 --> 00:09:34,519
this kind of situation in a dream, a game
or a story - so he will know what to do.

153
00:09:34,519 --> 00:09:38,389
So it's not that far fetched to hypothesise
that games dreams and stories have all three

154
00:09:38,389 --> 00:09:41,150
developed for the same purpose: Learning to
survive.

155
00:09:41,150 --> 00:09:47,050
So, once again, it all comes down to the most
powerful force on earth, the genetic imperative.

156
00:09:47,050 --> 00:09:50,930
As a rule of thumb, whenever earthlings enjoy
something, you can be pretty sure that the

157
00:09:50,930 --> 00:09:52,890
genetic imperative is behind it.

158
00:09:52,890 --> 00:09:57,700
In the next episode we will learn about estetics:
What is beauty, what is art, and why are humans

159
00:09:57,700 --> 00:09:59,240
so crazy about it.

160
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:22,029
Thanks for watching, and, as they say on Betelgeuse:
Don't forget to be alien!

